  "title": "How to make AI short films, from idea to final cut",
  "link": "https://picsart.com/blog/how-to-make-ai-short-films/",
  "pub_date": "Fri, 30 Oct 2026 13:25:58 +0000",
  "author": "Julia Tovmasyan",
  "content_snippet": "To make an AI short film, type your idea into Picsart’s AI short film maker, approve the story, cast and storyboards it builds, then download the finished cut as an MP4. The project moves through six stages: Idea, Story, Cast, Storyboards, Clips and Final film. You supply the premise, a visual style and a running … \nContinue reading \"How to make AI short films, from idea to final cut\"",
  "content_full": "<p><strong>To make an AI short film, type your idea into Picsart&#8217;s <a href=\"https://picsart.com/ai-short-films/\">AI short film maker</a>, approve the story, cast and storyboards it builds, then download the finished cut as an MP4.</strong> The project moves through six stages: Idea, Story, Cast, Storyboards, Clips and Final film. You supply the premise, a visual style and a running time anywhere from 15 seconds to 10 minutes. The rest is a series of review points where you decide what stays.</p>\n<p>The method holds together because each stage feeds the next. Character and location references come first, storyboards are drawn from those references, a continuity check reads the boards before anything moves, and an independent review grades the finished film. That order is what keeps a face, a costume or a prop steady from the first scene to the last.</p>\n<p>Below, you will find what makes a premise work at short length, what to check at each of the six stages, and how to continue a story once the first film is done.</p>\n<h2><span id=\"What_makes_an_AI_short_film_work\">What makes an AI short film work</span></h2>\n<p>Short films reward restraint. Every extra character, location or subplot is one more thing that has to stay consistent, so the strongest ideas stay small:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One idea you can say in a breath.</strong> A premise that fits in a single sentence gives the story writer a clear target.</li>\n<li><strong>A clear setup, escalation and payoff.</strong> Something goes wrong, someone acts, and the ending lands.</li>\n<li><strong>A small cast.</strong> Two or three characters create conflict without crowding the frame or multiplying reference sheets.</li>\n<li><strong>One or two locations.</strong> A tight setting gives the film a world that never drifts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Naming the tone inside the idea helps too, since words like &#8220;comedy&#8221; or &#8220;warm payoff&#8221; tell the writer what ending to build toward.</p>\n<h2><span id=\"What_you_can_make_with_the_AI_short_film_maker\">What you can make with the AI short film maker</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A short from one sentence.</strong> Turn a single concept into a finished cut of up to 10 minutes, and develop it through new versions.</li>\n<li><strong>A series with a returning cast.</strong> Number episodes by season, keep the same faces, and let each part pick up where the last one ended.</li>\n<li><strong>Vertical stories for social.</strong> Make 9:16 films from 15 seconds up, with a consistent cast and look that give an account a recognizable style.</li>\n<li><strong>Brand stories.</strong> Start from your own Library images, keep returning characters true to the brand, and hold one visual style across every film.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Longer films are built in episodes, which keeps a 10-minute story organized scene by scene.</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Make_AI_short_films_in_Picsart\">Make AI short films in Picsart</span></h2>\n<p>The full run lives inside Picsart&#8217;s AI short film maker, and a stepper at the top of the screen tracks each stage.</p>\n<section class=\"section_how_to\">\n    \n        <div class=\"how_to_steps\">\n                                        <div class=\"how_to_step how_to_step--highlighted\">\n                    <div class=\"how_to_step_content\">\n                        <p class=\"how_to_step_title\">\n                            <span class=\"how_to_step_number\">1.</span>\n                            Write the idea and set up the film                        </p>\n                                                    <p class=\"how_to_step_description\">Type two or three sentences into the &quot;Start with an Idea&quot; box: who the hero is, what goes wrong, and the tone of the ending. Add a title, pick one of eight styles, set a length from 15 seconds to 10 minutes, choose 9:16 or 16:9 and a dialogue language. Keep &quot;Review each step&quot; on for a first film and press Create story.</p>\n                                                                            <div class=\"how_to_cta_wrapper\">\n                                                                <input\n                                    type=\"file\"\n                                    id=\"how_to_upload_how-to-6ae4af0f2cc25_0\"\n                                    class=\"how_to_upload_input\"\n                                    accept=\"image/*\"\n                                    data-deeplink=\"https://picsart.com/ai-short-films/\"\n                                />\n                                <button\n                                    type=\"button\"\n                                    class=\"how_to_cta_button\"\n                                    data-upload-id=\"how_to_upload_how-to-6ae4af0f2cc25_0\"\n                                >\n                                    <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://cdn-cms-uploads.picsart.com/cms-uploads/9b784b6b-6f78-4ee4-a748-f4ad781bfd34.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" class=\"how_to_cta_icon\" />\n                                    <span>Create your film</span>\n                                </button>\n                                                            </div>\n                                            </div>\n                </div>\n                                                <div class=\"how_to_step how_to_step--highlighted\">\n                    <div class=\"how_to_step_content\">\n                        <p class=\"how_to_step_title\">\n                            <span class=\"how_to_step_number\">2.</span>\n                            Review and refine the story                        </p>\n                                                    <p class=\"how_to_step_description\">The writer returns a synopsis and a scene list with story beats and scene direction. Check that the setup and payoff survived and the cast stays small. Edit directly or type a change into &quot;Refine your story&quot;, then approve the story.</p>\n                                                                    </div>\n                </div>\n                                                <div class=\"how_to_step how_to_step--highlighted\">\n                    <div class=\"how_to_step_content\">\n                        <p class=\"how_to_step_title\">\n                            <span class=\"how_to_step_number\">3.</span>\n                            Approve the cast and location                        </p>\n                                                    <p class=\"how_to_step_description\">Each character gets a reference sheet with full-body views and face close-ups, plus a reference for the location. Check faces, outfits and props, adjust with the pencil icon or try another cast model, then approve.</p>\n                                                                    </div>\n                </div>\n                                                <div class=\"how_to_step how_to_step--highlighted\">\n                    <div class=\"how_to_step_content\">\n                        <p class=\"how_to_step_title\">\n                            <span class=\"how_to_step_number\">4.</span>\n                            Check storyboard continuity                        </p>\n                                                    <p class=\"how_to_step_description\">Each episode gets six frames, and a continuity check flags problems such as missing wardrobe or a prop in the wrong hands. Plan a targeted repair, redraw the set, or animate anyway. Fixing issues here is easier than fixing footage.</p>\n                                                                    </div>\n                </div>\n                                                <div class=\"how_to_step how_to_step--highlighted\">\n                    <div class=\"how_to_step_content\">\n                        <p class=\"how_to_step_title\">\n                            <span class=\"how_to_step_number\">5.</span>\n                            Animate the clips                        </p>\n                                                    <p class=\"how_to_step_description\">Approved storyboards become video clips. Ask for changes in plain language, such as &quot;slow the camera move&quot;, and only the selected clip is remade.</p>\n                                                                    </div>\n                </div>\n                                                <div class=\"how_to_step how_to_step--highlighted\">\n                    <div class=\"how_to_step_content\">\n                        <p class=\"how_to_step_title\">\n                            <span class=\"how_to_step_number\">6.</span>\n                            Review and download the final film                        </p>\n                                                    <p class=\"how_to_step_description\">The clips are assembled and an independent review scores the film, checking characters, wardrobe, props and dialogue, with timestamped notes. Keep the cut, redo a single scene or edit further, then press &quot;Download MP4&quot;.</p>\n                                                                    </div>\n                </div>\n                        </div>\n    </section>\n\n<script type=\"application/ld+json\">\n{\n    \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"HowTo\",\n    \"name\": \"\",\n    \"step\": [\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n            \"position\": 1,\n            \"name\": \"Write the idea and set up the film\",\n            \"text\": \"Type two or three sentences into the \\\"Start with an Idea\\\" box: who the hero is, what goes wrong, and the tone of the ending. Add a title, pick one of eight styles, set a length from 15 seconds to 10 minutes, choose 9:16 or 16:9 and a dialogue language. Keep \\\"Review each step\\\" on for a first film and press Create story.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n            \"position\": 2,\n            \"name\": \"Review and refine the story\",\n            \"text\": \"The writer returns a synopsis and a scene list with story beats and scene direction. Check that the setup and payoff survived and the cast stays small. Edit directly or type a change into \\\"Refine your story\\\", then approve the story.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n            \"position\": 3,\n            \"name\": \"Approve the cast and location\",\n            \"text\": \"Each character gets a reference sheet with full-body views and face close-ups, plus a reference for the location. Check faces, outfits and props, adjust with the pencil icon or try another cast model, then approve.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n            \"position\": 4,\n            \"name\": \"Check storyboard continuity\",\n            \"text\": \"Each episode gets six frames, and a continuity check flags problems such as missing wardrobe or a prop in the wrong hands. Plan a targeted repair, redraw the set, or animate anyway. Fixing issues here is easier than fixing footage.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n            \"position\": 5,\n            \"name\": \"Animate the clips\",\n            \"text\": \"Approved storyboards become video clips. Ask for changes in plain language, such as \\\"slow the camera move\\\", and only the selected clip is remade.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n            \"position\": 6,\n            \"name\": \"Review and download the final film\",\n            \"text\": \"The clips are assembled and an independent review scores the film, checking characters, wardrobe, props and dialogue, with timestamped notes. Keep the cut, redo a single scene or edit further, then press \\\"Download MP4\\\".\"\n        }\n    ]\n}</script>\n\n<script>\n(function() {\n    function uploadFallback(file) {\n        var UPLOAD_URL = 'https://upload.picsart.com/files';\n        var UPLOAD_URL_STAGE2 = 'https://upload-stage.picsartstage2.com/files';\n        var hostname = window.location.hostname;\n        var isStage2 = hostname.indexOf('picsartstage2.com') !== -1 || hostname.indexOf('stage2') !== -1;\n        var url = isStage2 ? UPLOAD_URL_STAGE2 : UPLOAD_URL;\n        var isSafari = /Safari/i.test(navigator.userAgent) && !/Chrome|Chromium|FxiOS|Edg|OPR/i.test(navigator.userAgent);\n        if (isSafari) {\n            return new Promise(function(resolve, reject) {\n                var formData = new FormData();\n                formData.append('type', 'editing-temp-landings');\n                formData.append('file', file);\n                formData.append('url', '');\n                formData.append('metainfo', '');\n                var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();\n                xhr.open('POST', url);\n                xhr.onload = function() {\n                    try {\n                        var data = xhr.responseText ? JSON.parse(xhr.responseText) : null;\n                        if (xhr.status >= 200 && xhr.status < 300 && data && data.result && data.result.url) {\n                            resolve(data.result.url);\n                        } else {\n                            reject(new Error('Upload failed'));\n                        }\n                    } catch (e) { reject(new Error('Upload failed')); }\n                };\n                xhr.onerror = xhr.ontimeout = function() { reject(new Error('Upload failed')); };\n                xhr.timeout = 60000;\n                xhr.send(formData);\n            });\n        }\n        var formData = new FormData();\n        formData.append('type', 'editing-temp-landings');\n        formData.append('file', file);\n        formData.append('url', '');\n        formData.append('metainfo', '');\n        return fetch(url, { method: 'POST', body: formData, mode: 'cors', cache: 'no-store' })\n            .then(function(res) { return res.text(); })\n            .then(function(text) {\n                try {\n                    var data = text ? JSON.parse(text) : null;\n                    if (data && data.result && data.result.url) return data.result.url;\n                } catch (e) { }\n                throw new Error('Upload failed');\n            });\n    }\n    var uploadFileToCDN = window.HowToUpload && window.HowToUpload.uploadFileToCDN\n        ? window.HowToUpload.uploadFileToCDN\n        : uploadFallback;\n\n    if (window._howToUploadBound) return;\n    window._howToUploadBound = true;\n\n    document.addEventListener('click', function(e) {\n        var btn = e.target && e.target.closest && e.target.closest('.how_to_cta_button');\n        if (!btn) return;\n        var uploadId = btn.getAttribute('data-upload-id');\n        var input = uploadId ? document.getElementById(uploadId) : null;\n        if (input) {\n            e.preventDefault();\n            input.click();\n        }\n    }, true);\n\n    document.addEventListener('change', function(e) {\n        if (!e.target || !e.target.classList || !e.target.classList.contains('how_to_upload_input')) return;\n        var file = e.target.files && e.target.files[0];\n        if (!file) return;\n        var inputEl = e.target;\n        var deeplink = inputEl.getAttribute('data-deeplink');\n        var button = document.querySelector('.how_to_cta_button[data-upload-id=\"' + inputEl.id + '\"]');\n        var labelSpan = button ? button.querySelector('span') : null;\n        var originalLabelText = labelSpan ? labelSpan.textContent : '';\n        if (button) {\n            button.disabled = true;\n            if (labelSpan) labelSpan.textContent = 'Uploading…';\n        }\n        uploadFileToCDN(file)\n            .then(function(cdnUrl) {\n                var separator = deeplink.indexOf('?') !== -1 ? '&' : '?';\n                var params = 'ref=blog&image=' + encodeURIComponent(cdnUrl);\n                var redirectUrl = deeplink + separator + params;\n                inputEl.value = '';\n                setTimeout(function() {\n                    window.location.assign(redirectUrl);\n                }, 0);\n            })\n            .catch(function() {\n                if (button) button.disabled = false;\n                if (labelSpan) labelSpan.textContent = originalLabelText;\n                inputEl.value = '';\n                alert('Upload failed. Please try again.');\n            });\n    }, true);\n})();\n</script>\n\n<h2><span id=\"Pick_the_writing_image_and_video_models\">Pick the writing, image and video models</span></h2>\n<p>The AI short film maker splits the work across three engines, and each one can be swapped on its own. A writing model drafts the story and dialogue, an <a href=\"https://picsart.com/image-models/\">image model</a> such as <a href=\"https://picsart.com/ai-models/gpt-image-2-5-sunburst/\">GPT Image 2.5 Sunburst</a> draws the characters, location, storyboards and film cover, and a <a href=\"https://picsart.com/video-models/\">video model</a> such as <a href=\"https://picsart.com/ai-models/minimax-h3-max/\">MiniMax H3 Max</a> animates the scenes. Set all three in the film settings before you press Create story. To see how a different model handles the same film, make a copy of the project: the earlier stages carry over, and the original version stays intact.</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Keep_the_story_going_with_Part_2_a_series_or_a_returning_cast\">Keep the story going with Part 2, a series or a returning cast</span></h2>\n<p>The final screen offers &#8220;Make Part 2&#8221;, and that is where a short becomes a franchise. Films can also be labeled by series, season and episode, or you can start a brand-new story with characters you already made. Selected character references are reused rather than generated again, so returning faces add no extra credits. The location can stay or change.</p>\n<section class=\"section_faq\" id=\"faq-faq-6ae4af0f2ce57\">\n            <h2 class=\"faq_title\" id=\"Get_answers_to_common_questions\">Get answers to common questions</h2>\n    \n    <div class=\"faq_items\">\n                    <div class=\"faq_item faq_item--active\">\n                <button type=\"button\" class=\"faq_question\" aria-expanded=\"true\">\n                    <span class=\"faq_question_text\">How long should an AI short film be?</span>\n                    <svg class=\"faq_chevron\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n                        <path d=\"M6 9L12 15L18 9\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n                    </svg>\n                </button>\n                <div class=\"faq_answer\" aria-hidden=\"false\">\n                    <div class=\"faq_answer_content\"><p>The AI short film maker creates films between 15 seconds and 10 minutes long, adjustable in 15-second steps. A 30-second cut splits into two short episodes, a good size for a first experiment.</p>\n</div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"faq_divider\"></div>\n            </div>\n                    <div class=\"faq_item \">\n                <button type=\"button\" class=\"faq_question\" aria-expanded=\"false\">\n                    <span class=\"faq_question_text\">Can AI make a whole short film out of a single idea?</span>\n                    <svg class=\"faq_chevron\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n                        <path d=\"M6 9L12 15L18 9\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n                    </svg>\n                </button>\n                <div class=\"faq_answer\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-collapsed>\n                    <div class=\"faq_answer_content\"><p>Yes. One sentence is enough to produce a synopsis, scenes, a cast, a location, storyboards, animated clips and an assembled film with a cover. You can review each stage or let it run automatically up to a credit limit you choose.</p>\n</div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"faq_divider\"></div>\n            </div>\n                    <div class=\"faq_item \">\n                <button type=\"button\" class=\"faq_question\" aria-expanded=\"false\">\n                    <span class=\"faq_question_text\">How do you keep characters consistent across scenes?</span>\n                    <svg class=\"faq_chevron\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n                        <path d=\"M6 9L12 15L18 9\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n                    </svg>\n                </button>\n                <div class=\"faq_answer\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-collapsed>\n                    <div class=\"faq_answer_content\"><p>Lock the look before any animation happens. Reference sheets fix each face and outfit, storyboards are drawn from those sheets, and a continuity check flags slips such as wardrobe changing between panels before clips are made. The final review then checks characters, wardrobe and props again.</p>\n</div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"faq_divider\"></div>\n            </div>\n                    <div class=\"faq_item \">\n                <button type=\"button\" class=\"faq_question\" aria-expanded=\"false\">\n                    <span class=\"faq_question_text\">How do you fix one scene in an AI short film without starting over?</span>\n                    <svg class=\"faq_chevron\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n                        <path d=\"M6 9L12 15L18 9\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n                    </svg>\n                </button>\n                <div class=\"faq_answer\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-collapsed>\n                    <div class=\"faq_answer_content\"><p>Describe the change in plain words, and only the piece you select is regenerated, be it a clip, a storyboard, a character, the location or the cover. The final film screen also offers a single-scene redo with a limited number of tries.</p>\n</div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"faq_divider\"></div>\n            </div>\n                    <div class=\"faq_item \">\n                <button type=\"button\" class=\"faq_question\" aria-expanded=\"false\">\n                    <span class=\"faq_question_text\">How do you make Part 2 of an AI short film?</span>\n                    <svg class=\"faq_chevron\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n                        <path d=\"M6 9L12 15L18 9\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n                    </svg>\n                </button>\n                <div class=\"faq_answer\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-collapsed>\n                    <div class=\"faq_answer_content\"><p>Press &#8220;Make Part 2&#8221; on the final film screen. The characters you pick are reused as they are, with no new reference sheets to generate, and you decide whether to keep the location or move somewhere new.</p>\n</div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"faq_divider\"></div>\n            </div>\n            </div>\n</section>\n\n<script type=\"application/ld+json\">\n{\n    \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n    \"mainEntity\": [\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"Question\",\n            \"name\": \"How long should an AI short film be?\",\n            \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                \"text\": \"The AI short film maker creates films between 15 seconds and 10 minutes long, adjustable in 15-second steps. A 30-second cut splits into two short episodes, a good size for a first experiment.\"\n            }\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"Question\",\n            \"name\": \"Can AI make a whole short film out of a single idea?\",\n            \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                \"text\": \"Yes. One sentence is enough to produce a synopsis, scenes, a cast, a location, storyboards, animated clips and an assembled film with a cover. You can review each stage or let it run automatically up to a credit limit you choose.\"\n            }\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"Question\",\n            \"name\": \"How do you keep characters consistent across scenes?\",\n            \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                \"text\": \"Lock the look before any animation happens. Reference sheets fix each face and outfit, storyboards are drawn from those sheets, and a continuity check flags slips such as wardrobe changing between panels before clips are made. The final review then checks characters, wardrobe and props again.\"\n            }\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"Question\",\n            \"name\": \"How do you fix one scene in an AI short film without starting over?\",\n            \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                \"text\": \"Describe the change in plain words, and only the piece you select is regenerated, be it a clip, a storyboard, a character, the location or the cover. The final film screen also offers a single-scene redo with a limited number of tries.\"\n            }\n        },\n        {\n            \"@type\": \"Question\",\n            \"name\": \"How do you make Part 2 of an AI short film?\",\n            \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                \"text\": \"Press &#8220;Make Part 2&#8221; on the final film screen. The characters you pick are reused as they are, with no new reference sheets to generate, and you decide whether to keep the location or move somewhere new.\"\n            }\n        }\n    ]\n}</script>\n\n<script>\n(function() {\n    var container = document.getElementById('faq-faq-6ae4af0f2ce57');\n    if (!container) return;\n\n    var items = container.querySelectorAll('.faq_item');\n    items.forEach(function(item) {\n        var button = item.querySelector('.faq_question');\n        var answer = item.querySelector('.faq_answer');\n        if (!button || !answer) return;\n\n        button.addEventListener('click', function() {\n            var isActive = item.classList.contains('faq_item--active');\n\n            if (isActive) {\n                item.classList.remove('faq_item--active');\n                button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');\n                answer.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');\n                answer.setAttribute('data-collapsed', '');\n            } else {\n                items.forEach(function(other) {\n                    var otherBtn = other.querySelector('.faq_question');\n                    var otherAnswer = other.querySelector('.faq_answer');\n                    other.classList.remove('faq_item--active');\n                    if (otherBtn) otherBtn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');\n                    if (otherAnswer) {\n                        otherAnswer.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');\n                        otherAnswer.setAttribute('data-collapsed', '');\n                    }\n                });\n                item.classList.add('faq_item--active');\n                button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'true');\n                answer.removeAttribute('data-collapsed');\n                answer.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');\n            }\n        });\n    });\n})();\n</script>\n\n<h2><span id=\"Start_your_first_AI_short_film\">Start your first AI short film</span></h2>\n<p>Every film starts as a few sentences in a text box. Write yours, pick a style and a length, and let the six stages turn it into a finished cut. Open the AI short film maker and put your idea on screen today.</p>\n",
